  <h2>Starting from zero: the first 90 days</h2>
  <p>
    Say you are a firm doing $1M to $2M a year with no marketing at all. The order of
    operations we would run: direct response ads, Google or Facebook, and if you have to
    pick one, pick Facebook, more cost-efficient and easier to learn if you are doing
    this yourself. Honestly, at that revenue the better move is finding an agency you
    trust to run it, and it does not have to be us. The plan below works either way.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run a simple book-a-call funnel: square image ads into a Facebook form that asks
    about timeframe, budget and location. Call every lead fast, speed matters more than
    polish here, qualify on that first phone call, then move the real ones to a video
    consultation and a proposal review. Two calls, not a courtship.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not start with less than $100 a day. And if you are running it yourself, set
    aside $5K to $10K you are fully prepared to waste, because paid ads are learned by
    execution, not theory. Money you have already written off cannot scare you into
    emotional decisions; you read the numbers instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    What to skip: cold outreach, which is inefficient for a purchase this size, and
    magazine ads, because it is not 1932.
  </p>
  <p>
    In parallel, start the organic engine, because organic feeds your paid numbers: the
    stronger your presence, the cheaper everything else gets. Instagram first, YouTube
    second, two videos a week if you can manage it: client interviews, walkthroughs of
    finished projects with the owners in them, answers to the questions your buyers
    actually ask.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then expect nothing from it for six to twelve months. That is the normal timeline,
    which is exactly why the ads run alongside it. The deeper playbooks are <a href="/content-marketing-for-architects">content marketing for architects</a> and <a href="/video-marketing-for-architects">video marketing for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How much should an architecture firm spend on marketing?</h2>
  <p>
    The number everyone repeats is 3 to 5 percent of revenue, maybe 10 if you are
    feeling brave. We think percent-of-revenue rules are how small firms talk themselves
    out of growing. Judge spend by what it returns. If $30K of marketing brings back
    $100K in signed work, stop calling it overspending and go feed the machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The percentage matters far less than two questions most firms cannot answer: are you
    spending it on purpose, and do you know what comes back?
  </p>
  <p>
    One reframe that ends a lot of budget arguments: your website does not go out and
    find anyone. It sits there and makes the people who already found you feel safe
    about inquiring. Unless you are making a serious push on search, do not fund a fancy
    website before you fund the thing that brings people to it.
  </p>

  <h2>The traps that look like marketing</h2>
  <p>
    The pattern under most of these: they are credibility plays, and credibility only
    pays once people already know you. A firm under $3M a year is essentially invisible,
    no third-party reviews, no reputation to polish, and that is fine. Direct response
    first, revenue up, then the credibility layer starts earning its keep.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Awards and magazine features as a plan. Lovely for the wall, and worth having as proof, but your buyers are not reading the trade press, they are on their phones. More on using <a href="/pr-and-press-for-architects#what-press-actually-buys-you">PR and press for architects</a> as proof instead of a pipeline.</li>
    <li>Houzz and the lead marketplaces, which get lumped together and should not be. Houzz sells advertising, so you are paying for placement in front of its own audience, and the <a href="https://www.houzz.com/magazine/2026-u-s-houzz-and-home-study-renovation-trends-stsetivw-vs~185090855" rel="nofollow">median renovation that audience ran last year was $20,000</a>. The pay-per-lead sites sell the same contact to several firms at once, which turns into a race to the cheapest fee. The big project is not coming out of either pipe. The full arithmetic on that platform, including what its advertising costs, is in <a href="/houzz-for-architects#the-10-000-problem">Houzz for architects</a>.</li>
    <li>The boost button. Meta's "boost" buys you likes from people who will never hire you and calls it a campaign.</li>
    <li>One brand, one message, two completely different buyers. Residential warmth pitched at a developer reads as fluff. Commercial spreadsheet pitched at a nervous first-timer reads as cold. What identity work can and cannot fix in the first place is <a href="/branding-for-architects#what-branding-for-architecture-firms-can-fix-and-what-it-can">branding for architects</a>.</li>
    <li>Measuring nothing, so the channel that quietly works gets cut alongside the one that quietly does not.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>What is architecture marketing?</h3>
  <p>
    Getting the right buyers to find you on purpose, then turning that attention into signed
    projects, instead of waiting to be recommended. Everything else is detail.
  </p>
  <h3>What are the best marketing strategies for architects?</h3>
  <p>
    The ones that create demand instead of waiting for it. In rough order of how fast they
    pay: paid social to put your work in front of buyers who are not searching yet, a website
    with one obvious next step, follow-up that runs without you, then search and content
    underneath as the layer that compounds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Referrals and partnerships sit alongside all of it, run as a system rather than luck. Which you start with depends on who you sell to,
    homeowners if you do residential and developers or business owners if you do commercial,
    and the table above splits every channel by the job it does.
  </p>
  <h3>How do architects attract clients?</h3>
  <p>
    By being visible before the buyer starts looking, then making it easy to raise a hand.
    Most firms run that backwards, waiting to be recommended and then wondering why the
    pipeline moves at somebody else's pace. What typically works fastest for us is paid
    social with a simple form, because it reaches people who are planning a project and have
    not typed anything into Google yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the residential side that is a homeowner nine months from breaking ground. On the commercial side it is a developer quietly building a
    shortlist you are not on. The full version of that pipeline is <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects#what-a-system-actually-looks-like">lead generation for architects</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>How do you write a marketing plan for an architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    Start from a number rather than a channel. Decide how many signed projects you want over
    the next twelve months, work back to how many consultations that takes at your close
    rate, then pick the smallest set of channels that can produce those conversations.
  </p>
  <p>
    One demand channel, one conversion asset, one follow-up sequence is a real plan. Write down
    what you will measure and when you will judge it, because the most common way these
    plans die is a channel getting cut at week six when it needed twelve.
  </p>
  <h3>Isn't marketing a bit beneath the profession?</h3>
  <p>
    The firm that believes that is funding the one that doesn't. Marketing is not begging for
    work. It is making sure the people who need exactly what you do can actually find you
    before they hand the project to someone worse who simply showed up first.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I start marketing my architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    Decide which buyer you want more of, residential or commercial, then read that page and fix
    your website's next step. Visibility is wasted if the page it points to is a dead end. And if you would rather hire help than run it yourself, start with the ranking of the <a href="/best-marketing-agencies-for-architects">top marketing agencies for architecture firms</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Do architecture firms need a marketing agency?</h3>
  <p>
    No, and plenty of firms run this in-house perfectly well. What an agency buys you is
    reps: someone who has run the same play across dozens of firms and already knows which
    offers and which creative tend to work in this market, so you skip the expensive half of
    learning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Under roughly $1M a year, with more time than money, running it yourself is
    reasonable if you set aside five to ten thousand dollars you are prepared to lose while
    you learn. Above that, the hours you spend inside an ad account usually cost more than
    the fee. If you do go looking, how you pay matters more than the logo, and the three
    models are compared in <a href="/pay-per-lead-vs-retainer-for-architects">pay per lead versus retainer</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Stop marketing by accident</h2>
  <p>
    That is the whole thing. Pick the buyer, get found on purpose, convert the attention, earn
    trust, follow up, measure, repeat. Most firms do none of it and call the result "how the
    industry works." If you would rather have the system built and run for you than reverse
    engineer it between deadlines, that is the part we handle for architecture firms.
  </p>
